The model
---------

The classifier model is derived from BEREL 3.0 by DICTA: The Israel Center for Text Analysis
(https://huggingface.co/dicta-il/BEREL_3.0), licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0.
Changes: fine-tuned to classify divine names, vocabulary pruned, and quantized (8-bit layers,
4-bit embedding table). DICTA does not endorse this project.

BEREL is described in: Avi Shmidman, Joshua Guedalia, Shaltiel Shmidman, Cheyn Shmuel
Shmidman, Eli Handel and Moshe Koppel, "Introducing BEREL: BERT Embeddings for
Rabbinic-Encoded Language", arXiv:2208.01875, 2022.


Training data
-------------

The model was trained on the texts below. They were used as training data only: none of them
is distributed with Genizah.

- עולה מן המדבר (Olah Min HaMidbar), the journal of ישיבת ברכת משה (Yeshivat Birkat Moshe),
  used with the yeshiva's permission. Our thanks to the journal's editors over the years,
  whose careful censoring of divine names by hand, issue after issue, is what the model
  learned from.
- Open Scriptures Hebrew Bible (OSHB), https://github.com/openscriptures/morphhb, CC BY 4.0.
  Its text is the Westminster Leningrad Codex, public domain.
- Translators Amalgamated Hebrew OT (TAHOT), STEPBible.org, Tyndale House, Cambridge,
  https://github.com/STEPBible/STEPBible-Data, CC BY 4.0.
- Vocalized liturgy from Sefaria, https://www.sefaria.org, in these editions:
  - CC BY: The Metsudah Siddur (Avrohom Davis, Metsudah Publications, 1981), in the Siddur
    Ashkenaz, Siddur Sefard and Linear Siddur editions; The Metsudah Machzor for Rosh
    Hashanah and Yom Kippur (Metsudah Publications, New York); The Metsudah Selichos
    (Metsudah Publications, 1986).
  - CC BY-SA: Selichot Nusach Lita, Selichot Nusach Polin, Kinnot for Tisha B'Av and Lekha
    Dodi from Wikisource; Selichot, Nusach Chabad, based on text from Wikisource; Yedid
    Nefesh, original version with nikkud; Shalom Aleichem; Leshon Chakhamim, Bakhshi
    Edition, 2021.
  - Public domain or CC0: Torat Emet, Daat Siddur Ashkenaz, the Shaliehsaboo edition of
    Siddur Edot HaMizrach, and other public-domain editions.
- Medieval Hebrew poetry from Project Ben-Yehuda (https://benyehuda.org), public domain,
  thanks to the Project Ben-Yehuda volunteers.


